{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017vnVhKSJPf77jfW29YywsW","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd005-wc\n     2→title: \"cmd/wc: Count Lines, Words, and Bytes\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  Scripts and pipelines frequently need line, word, or byte counts from files or stdin.\n     6→  The macOS BSD wc differs from GNU wc in flag interactions and column alignment, making\n     7→  it an unreliable reference on macOS. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU\n     8→  reference binary (gwc, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations\n     9→  and multi-file output, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n    10→\n    11→goals:\n    12→  - G1: Count newlines, words, and bytes from one or more files or stdin, matching GNU wc output format.\n    13→  - G2: Support the -c, -l, -m, -w, and -L flags and their combinations, including the default (no flags) behavior.\n    14→  - G3: Print a totals line when more than one file is given.\n    15→  - G4: Produce column-aligned output when counts from multiple files are printed.\n    16→\n    17→requirements:\n    18→  R1:\n    19→    title: Default behavior (no flags)\n    20→    items:\n    21→      - R1.1: When no flags are given, must print line count, word count, and byte count (in that order) for each input, matching the wc.c default (print_lines = print_words = print_bytes = true; set at wc.c:862).\n    22→      - R1.2: Must read from stdin when no file arguments are given. Must read from each named file in order when file arguments are given.\n    23→      - R1.3: Output for each file must be on a single line with counts followed by the filename. For stdin input, no filename is printed.\n    24→      - R1.4: When more than one file is given, must print a final \"total\" line with the sum of all counts.\n    25→\n    26→  R2:\n    27→    title: Flag behavior\n    28→    items:\n    29→      - R2.1: -l must count newline characters (not logical lines). A file with no trailing newline has a newline count equal to the number of complete lines.\n    30→      - R2.2: -w must count words, where a word is a maximal sequence of non-whitespace characters. Whitespace is determined by the locale-sensitive isspace() equivalent in Go (unicode.IsSpace).\n    31→      - R2.3: -c must count bytes. -c and -m are mutually exclusive in output but may be passed together; -m takes precedence when both are given (matching wc.c behavior where print_chars overrides print_bytes display).\n    32→      - R2.4: -m must count characters (Unicode code points), not bytes. When the input is valid UTF-8, this is the rune count. When the input contains invalid UTF-8, each invalid byte counts as one character.\n    33→      - R2.5: -L must print the length of the longest line in the input, measured in display columns (tab-expanded, treating each tab as advancing to the next multiple of 8). -L is independent of other flags and may be combined with any of them.\n    34→      - R2.6: 'When flags are combined, must print each requested count in the order: lines (-l), words (-w), chars (-m) or bytes (-c), max-line-length (-L). This is the fixed output column order from wc.c:237-247, not the flag order given by the user.'\n    35→\n    36→  R3:\n    37→    title: Multi-file output and column alignment\n    38→    items:\n    39→      - R3.1: When multiple files are given, each line must right-align counts in columns wide enough for the largest count in that column across all files. This matches GNU wc's dynamic column-width behavior.\n    40→      - R3.2: The total line must use the label \"total\" (not a filename). The counts in the total line must be the arithmetic sum of counts from all files.\n    41→      - R3.3: Must support --total=auto (default), --total=always (print total even for one file), --total=only (print only the total, not per-file lines), and --total=never (never print total, even for multiple files).\n    42→\n    43→  R4:\n    44→    title: Stdin and special inputs\n    45→    items:\n    46→      - R4.1: Must treat \"-\" as a filename argument meaning stdin, consistent with POSIX convention.\n    47→      - R4.2: Must handle binary input (arbitrary byte sequences) without corrupting output or panicking.\n    48→      - R4.3: Must handle empty input (zero bytes) and produce \"0 0 0\" (or the selected subset of counts) correctly.\n    49→      - R4.4: Must support --files0-from=FILE which reads NUL-delimited filenames from FILE and processes each. When FILE is \"-\", filenames are read from stdin.\n    50→\n    51→  R5:\n    52→    title: Locale handling\n    53→    items:\n    54→      - R5.1: Differential tests must set LC_ALL=C to avoid locale-dependent divergence between the Go and reference binary outputs for -m (character count). Document this constraint in the test suite.\n    55→      - R5.2: Under LC_ALL=C, -m and -c must produce identical counts (each byte counts as one character). The Go implementation must produce the same result.\n    56→\n    57→  R6:\n    58→    title: Exit codes\n    59→    items:\n    60→      - R6.1: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully.\n    61→      - R6.2: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened or read. Must still process and print counts for the files that were successfully read before exiting 1.\n    62→      - R6.3: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout.\n    63→\n    64→non_goals:\n    65→  - cmd/wc does not implement -c with block-size adjustments (those belong to du).\n    66→  - cmd/wc does not implement color output.\n    67→  - cmd/wc does not implement wide-character display-width handling for -L beyond tab expansion; East Asian wide characters are out of scope for the initial implementation.\n    68→\n    69→acceptance_criteria:\n    70→  - \"AC1: printf 'foo\\\\nbar baz\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/wc produces '2 3 12', matching gwc on the same input.\"\n    71→  - \"AC2: go run ./cmd/wc -l -w file1 file2 produces right-aligned counts and a correct 'total' line, matching gwc output byte-for-byte.\"\n    72→  - \"AC3: Differential test against gwc passes on default mode, -l, -w, -c, -m (with LC_ALL=C), -L, multi-file, and stdin modes.\"\n    73→  - \"AC4: go run ./cmd/wc on a non-existent file prints an error to stderr, exits 1, and still processes any other files on the command line.\"\n    74→  - \"AC5: Empty input produces '0 0 0' (default flags), matching gwc.\"\n    75→  - \"AC6: cmd/wc compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    76→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"93994a83-5d4b-42ba-9a8f-7c6195384f3d","uuid":"f5424fda-ed49-4407-8b50-7d671dbc7179","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/241/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd005-wc.yaml","content":"id: prd005-wc\ntitle: \"cmd/wc: Count Lines, Words, and Bytes\"\n\nproblem: |\n  Scripts and pipelines frequently need line, word, or byte counts from files or stdin.\n  The macOS BSD wc differs from GNU wc in flag interactions and column alignment, making\n  it an unreliable reference on macOS. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU\n  reference binary (gwc, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations\n  and multi-file output, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Count newlines, words, and bytes from one or more files or stdin, matching GNU wc output format.\n  - G2: Support the -c, -l, -m, -w, and -L flags and their combinations, including the default (no flags) behavior.\n  - G3: Print a totals line when more than one file is given.\n  - G4: Produce column-aligned output when counts from multiple files are printed.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Default behavior (no flags)\n    items:\n      - R1.1: When no flags are given, must print line count, word count, and byte count (in that order) for each input, matching the wc.c default (print_lines = print_words = print_bytes = true; set at wc.c:862).\n      - R1.2: Must read from stdin when no file arguments are given. Must read from each named file in order when file arguments are given.\n      - R1.3: Output for each file must be on a single line with counts followed by the filename. For stdin input, no filename is printed.\n      - R1.4: When more than one file is given, must print a final \"total\" line with the sum of all counts.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Flag behavior\n    items:\n      - R2.1: -l must count newline characters (not logical lines). A file with no trailing newline has a newline count equal to the number of complete lines.\n      - R2.2: -w must count words, where a word is a maximal sequence of non-whitespace characters. Whitespace is determined by the locale-sensitive isspace() equivalent in Go (unicode.IsSpace).\n      - R2.3: -c must count bytes. -c and -m are mutually exclusive in output but may be passed together; -m takes precedence when both are given (matching wc.c behavior where print_chars overrides print_bytes display).\n      - R2.4: -m must count characters (Unicode code points), not bytes. When the input is valid UTF-8, this is the rune count. When the input contains invalid UTF-8, each invalid byte counts as one character.\n      - R2.5: -L must print the length of the longest line in the input, measured in display columns (tab-expanded, treating each tab as advancing to the next multiple of 8). -L is independent of other flags and may be combined with any of them.\n      - R2.6: 'When flags are combined, must print each requested count in the order: lines (-l), words (-w), chars (-m) or bytes (-c), max-line-length (-L). This is the fixed output column order from wc.c:237-247, not the flag order given by the user.'\n\n  R3:\n    title: Multi-file output and column alignment\n    items:\n      - R3.1: When multiple files are given, each line must right-align counts in columns wide enough for the largest count in that column across all files. This matches GNU wc's dynamic column-width behavior.\n      - R3.2: The total line must use the label \"total\" (not a filename). The counts in the total line must be the arithmetic sum of counts from all files.\n      - R3.3: Must support --total=auto (default), --total=always (print total even for one file), --total=only (print only the total, not per-file lines), and --total=never (never print total, even for multiple files).\n\n  R4:\n    title: Stdin and special inputs\n    items:\n      - R4.1: Must treat \"-\" as a filename argument meaning stdin, consistent with POSIX convention.\n      - R4.2: Must handle binary input (arbitrary byte sequences) without corrupting output or panicking.\n      - R4.3: Must handle empty input (zero bytes) and produce \"0 0 0\" (or the selected subset of counts) correctly.\n      - R4.4: Must support --files0-from=FILE which reads NUL-delimited filenames from FILE and processes each. When FILE is \"-\", filenames are read from stdin.\n\n  R5:\n    title: Locale handling\n    items:\n      - R5.1: Differential tests must set LC_ALL=C to avoid locale-dependent divergence between the Go and reference binary outputs for -m (character count). Document this constraint in the test suite.\n      - R5.2: Under LC_ALL=C, -m and -c must produce identical counts (each byte counts as one character). The Go implementation must produce the same result.\n\n  R6:\n    title: Exit codes\n    items:\n      - R6.1: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully.\n      - R6.2: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened or read. Must still process and print counts for the files that were successfully read before exiting 1.\n      - R6.3: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - cmd/wc does not implement -c with block-size adjustments (those belong to du).\n  - cmd/wc does not implement color output.\n  - cmd/wc does not implement wide-character display-width handling for -L beyond tab expansion; East Asian wide characters are out of scope for the initial implementation.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: printf 'foo\\\\nbar baz\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/wc produces '2 3 12', matching gwc on the same input.\"\n  - \"AC2: go run ./cmd/wc -l -w file1 file2 produces right-aligned counts and a correct 'total' line, matching gwc output byte-for-byte.\"\n  - \"AC3: Differential test against gwc passes on default mode, -l, -w, -c, -m (with LC_ALL=C), -L, multi-file, and stdin modes.\"\n  - \"AC4: go run ./cmd/wc on a non-existent file prints an error to stderr, exits 1, and still processes any other files on the command line.\"\n  - \"AC5: Empty input produces '0 0 0' (default flags), matching gwc.\"\n  - \"AC6: cmd/wc compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n","numLines":76,"startLine":1,"totalLines":76}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Nc56uCCt4FHK8LcGrGXpiR","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: test-rel03.0\n     2→title: \"Test Suite: rel03.0 — wc specification and differential test\"\n     3→release: rel03.0\n     4→\n     5→traces:\n     6→  - rel03.0-uc001-wc\n     7→  - prd005-wc R1\n     8→  - prd005-wc R2\n     9→  - prd005-wc R3\n    10→  - prd005-wc R4\n    11→  - prd005-wc R5\n    12→  - prd005-wc R6\n    13→\n    14→preconditions:\n    15→  - ./bin/wc exists (built via mage build).\n    16→  - Reference binary gwc is installed via Homebrew (coreutils) and on PATH.\n    17→  - Tests are run via go test ./cmd/wc/... which invokes RunDiffTests from pkg/testutils.\n    18→  - All tests use LC_ALL=C to eliminate locale-dependent divergence.\n    19→  - \"wc: no normalization is applied; wc output is deterministic integer counts.\"\n    20→\n    21→test_cases:\n    22→  - name: wc_default_three_lines\n    23→    description: Default flags (line, word, byte count) with three-line stdin.\n    24→    inputs:\n    25→      args: []\n    26→      stdin: \"foo\\nbar baz\\nqux\\n\"\n    27→      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    28→    normalization: none\n    29→    expected:\n    30→      exit_code: 0\n    31→      stdout_structure: 'output contains \"3\"'\n    32→      stderr: \"\"\n    33→    traces:\n    34→      - prd005-wc R1.1\n    35→      - prd005-wc R1.2\n    36→      - prd005-wc R1.3\n    37→\n    38→  - name: wc_lines_only\n    39→    description: -l flag counts newline characters only.\n    40→    inputs:\n    41→      args: [\"-l\"]\n    42→      stdin: \"one\\ntwo\\nthree\\n\"\n    43→      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    44→    normalization: none\n    45→    expected:\n    46→      exit_code: 0\n    47→      stdout_structure: 'output contains \"3\"'\n    48→      stderr: \"\"\n    49→    traces:\n    50→      - prd005-wc R2.1\n    51→      - prd005-wc R2.6\n    52→\n    53→  - name: wc_words_only\n    54→    description: -w flag counts words (maximal sequences of non-whitespace).\n    55→    inputs:\n    56→      args: [\"-w\"]\n    57→      stdin: \"hello world\\ngoodbye  cruel   world\\n\"\n    58→      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    59→    normalization: none\n    60→    expected:\n    61→      exit_code: 0\n    62→      stdout_structure: 'output contains \"5\"'\n    63→      stderr: \"\"\n    64→    traces:\n    65→      - prd005-wc R2.2\n    66→      - prd005-wc R2.6\n    67→\n    68→  - name: wc_bytes_only\n    69→    description: -c flag counts bytes.\n    70→    inputs:\n    71→      args: [\"-c\"]\n    72→      stdin: \"abc\\n\"\n    73→      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    74→    normalization: none\n    75→    expected:\n    76→      exit_code: 0\n    77→      stdout_structure: 'output contains \"4\"'\n    78→      stderr: \"\"\n    79→    traces:\n    80→      - prd005-wc R2.3\n    81→\n    82→  - name: wc_chars_lc_c\n    83→    description: -m with LC_ALL=C; character count equals byte count under C locale.\n    84→    inputs:\n    85→      args: [\"-m\"]\n    86→      stdin: \"hello\\n\"\n    87→      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    88→    normalization: none\n    89→    expected:\n    90→      exit_code: 0\n    91→      stdout_structure: 'output contains \"6\"'\n    92→      stderr: \"\"\n    93→    traces:\n    94→      - prd005-wc R2.4\n    95→      - prd005-wc R5.1\n    96→      - prd005-wc R5.2\n    97→\n    98→  - name: wc_max_line_length\n    99→    description: -L flag reports the longest line length with tab expansion.\n   100→    inputs:\n   101→      args: [\"-L\"]\n   102→      stdin: \"short\\na much longer line here\\nmed\\n\"\n   103→      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n   104→    normalization: none\n   105→    expected:\n   106→      exit_code: 0\n   107→      stdout_structure: 'output contains \"22\"'\n   108→      stderr: \"\"\n   109→    traces:\n   110→      - prd005-wc R2.5\n   111→\n   112→  - name: wc_combined_flags_order\n   113→    description: Combined flags (-l -w -c) produce counts in fixed order (lines, words, bytes).\n   114→    inputs:\n   115→      args: [\"-w\", \"-l\", \"-c\"]\n   116→      stdin: \"one two\\nthree\\n\"\n   117→      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n   118→    normalization: none\n   119→    expected:\n   120→      exit_code: 0\n   121→      stdout_structure: counts in order lines, words, bytes regardless of flag order\n   122→      stderr: \"\"\n   123→    traces:\n   124→      - prd005-wc R2.6\n   125→\n   126→  - name: wc_multi_file_total\n   127→    description: 'Two files produce per-file lines and a total line with right-aligned columns. Setup: file1.txt contains \"hello\\nworld\\n\" (2 lines, 2 words, 12 bytes), file2.txt contains \"foo bar baz\\n\" (1 line, 3 words, 12 bytes)'\n   128→    inputs:\n   129→      args: [\"file1.txt\", \"file2.txt\"]\n   130→      stdin: null\n   131→      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n   132→    normalization: none\n   133→    expected:\n   134→      exit_code: 0\n   135→      stdout_structure: three lines — file1 counts, file2 counts, total with label \"total\"; columns right-aligned\n   136→      stderr: \"\"\n   137→    traces:\n   138→      - prd005-wc R1.4\n   139→      - prd005-wc R3.1\n   140→      - prd005-wc R3.2\n   141→\n   142→  - name: wc_total_only\n   143→    description: '--total=only with two files prints only the total line. Setup: file1.txt contains \"a\\n\", file2.txt contains \"b\\nc\\n\"'\n   144→    inputs:\n   145→      args: [\"--total=only\", \"file1.txt\", \"file2.txt\"]\n   146→      stdin: null\n   147→      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n   148→    normalization: none\n   149→    expected:\n   150→      exit_code: 0\n   151→      stdout_structure: one line with summed counts and label \"total\"\n   152→      stderr: \"\"\n   153→    traces:\n   154→      - prd005-wc R3.3\n   155→\n   156→  - name: wc_total_never\n   157→    description: '--total=never with two files prints no total line. Setup: file1.txt contains \"x\\n\", file2.txt contains \"y\\n\"'\n   158→    inputs:\n   159→      args: [\"--total=never\", \"file1.txt\", \"file2.txt\"]\n   160→      stdin: null\n   161→      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n   162→    normalization: none\n   163→    expected:\n   164→      exit_code: 0\n   165→      stdout_structure: two lines (one per file), no total line\n   166→      stderr: \"\"\n   167→    traces:\n   168→      - prd005-wc R3.3\n   169→\n   170→  - name: wc_stdin_dash\n   171→    description: '\"-\" as a filename argument reads from stdin.'\n   172→    inputs:\n   173→      args: [\"-\"]\n   174→      stdin: \"stdin content\\n\"\n   175→      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n   176→    normalization: none\n   177→    expected:\n   178→      exit_code: 0\n   179→      stdout_structure: one line of counts (no filename label for stdin dash)\n   180→      stderr: \"\"\n   181→    traces:\n   182→      - prd005-wc R4.1\n   183→\n   184→  - name: wc_empty_stdin\n   185→    description: Zero bytes of stdin produces \"0 0 0\" for default flags.\n   186→    inputs:\n   187→      args: []\n   188→      stdin: \"\"\n   189→      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n   190→    normalization: none\n   191→    expected:\n   192→      exit_code: 0\n   193→      stdout_structure: 'output contains \"0\"'\n   194→      stderr: \"\"\n   195→    traces:\n   196→      - prd005-wc R4.3\n   197→\n   198→  - name: wc_missing_file\n   199→    description: 'Non-existent file prints error to stderr, exits 1, processes remaining files. Setup: real.txt contains \"data\\n\", nonexistent.txt does not exist'\n   200→    inputs:\n   201→      args: [\"nonexistent.txt\", \"real.txt\"]\n   202→      stdin: null\n   203→      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n   204→    normalization: none\n   205→    expected:\n   206→      exit_code: 1\n   207→      stdout_structure: counts for real.txt and a total line are printed\n   208→      stderr_contains: \"nonexistent.txt\"\n   209→    traces:\n   210→      - prd005-wc R6.2\n   211→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"93994a83-5d4b-42ba-9a8f-7c6195384f3d","uuid":"8ae90ffc-489b-4f49-a801-b06e388481fb","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/241/docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel03.0.yaml","content":"id: test-rel03.0\ntitle: \"Test Suite: rel03.0 — wc specification and differential test\"\nrelease: rel03.0\n\ntraces:\n  - rel03.0-uc001-wc\n  - prd005-wc R1\n  - prd005-wc R2\n  - prd005-wc R3\n  - prd005-wc R4\n  - prd005-wc R5\n  - prd005-wc R6\n\npreconditions:\n  - ./bin/wc exists (built via mage build).\n  - Reference binary gwc is installed via Homebrew (coreutils) and on PATH.\n  - Tests are run via go test ./cmd/wc/... which invokes RunDiffTests from pkg/testutils.\n  - All tests use LC_ALL=C to eliminate locale-dependent divergence.\n  - \"wc: no normalization is applied; wc output is deterministic integer counts.\"\n\ntest_cases:\n  - name: wc_default_three_lines\n    description: Default flags (line, word, byte count) with three-line stdin.\n    inputs:\n      args: []\n      stdin: \"foo\\nbar baz\\nqux\\n\"\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: 'output contains \"3\"'\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd005-wc R1.1\n      - prd005-wc R1.2\n      - prd005-wc R1.3\n\n  - name: wc_lines_only\n    description: -l flag counts newline characters only.\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"-l\"]\n      stdin: \"one\\ntwo\\nthree\\n\"\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: 'output contains \"3\"'\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd005-wc R2.1\n      - prd005-wc R2.6\n\n  - name: wc_words_only\n    description: -w flag counts words (maximal sequences of non-whitespace).\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"-w\"]\n      stdin: \"hello world\\ngoodbye  cruel   world\\n\"\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: 'output contains \"5\"'\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd005-wc R2.2\n      - prd005-wc R2.6\n\n  - name: wc_bytes_only\n    description: -c flag counts bytes.\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"-c\"]\n      stdin: \"abc\\n\"\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: 'output contains \"4\"'\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd005-wc R2.3\n\n  - name: wc_chars_lc_c\n    description: -m with LC_ALL=C; character count equals byte count under C locale.\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"-m\"]\n      stdin: \"hello\\n\"\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: 'output contains \"6\"'\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd005-wc R2.4\n      - prd005-wc R5.1\n      - prd005-wc R5.2\n\n  - name: wc_max_line_length\n    description: -L flag reports the longest line length with tab expansion.\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"-L\"]\n      stdin: \"short\\na much longer line here\\nmed\\n\"\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: 'output contains \"22\"'\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd005-wc R2.5\n\n  - name: wc_combined_flags_order\n    description: Combined flags (-l -w -c) produce counts in fixed order (lines, words, bytes).\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"-w\", \"-l\", \"-c\"]\n      stdin: \"one two\\nthree\\n\"\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: counts in order lines, words, bytes regardless of flag order\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd005-wc R2.6\n\n  - name: wc_multi_file_total\n    description: 'Two files produce per-file lines and a total line with right-aligned columns. Setup: file1.txt contains \"hello\\nworld\\n\" (2 lines, 2 words, 12 bytes), file2.txt contains \"foo bar baz\\n\" (1 line, 3 words, 12 bytes)'\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"file1.txt\", \"file2.txt\"]\n      stdin: null\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: three lines — file1 counts, file2 counts, total with label \"total\"; columns right-aligned\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd005-wc R1.4\n      - prd005-wc R3.1\n      - prd005-wc R3.2\n\n  - name: wc_total_only\n    description: '--total=only with two files prints only the total line. Setup: file1.txt contains \"a\\n\", file2.txt contains \"b\\nc\\n\"'\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"--total=only\", \"file1.txt\", \"file2.txt\"]\n      stdin: null\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: one line with summed counts and label \"total\"\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd005-wc R3.3\n\n  - name: wc_total_never\n    description: '--total=never with two files prints no total line. Setup: file1.txt contains \"x\\n\", file2.txt contains \"y\\n\"'\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"--total=never\", \"file1.txt\", \"file2.txt\"]\n      stdin: null\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: two lines (one per file), no total line\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd005-wc R3.3\n\n  - name: wc_stdin_dash\n    description: '\"-\" as a filename argument reads from stdin.'\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"-\"]\n      stdin: \"stdin content\\n\"\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: one line of counts (no filename label for stdin dash)\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd005-wc R4.1\n\n  - name: wc_empty_stdin\n    description: Zero bytes of stdin produces \"0 0 0\" for default flags.\n    inputs:\n      args: []\n      stdin: \"\"\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: 'output contains \"0\"'\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd005-wc R4.3\n\n  - name: wc_missing_file\n    description: 'Non-existent file prints error to stderr, exits 1, processes remaining files. Setup: real.txt contains \"data\\n\", nonexistent.txt does not exist'\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"nonexistent.txt\", \"real.txt\"]\n      stdin: null\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 1\n      stdout_structure: counts for real.txt and a total line are printed\n      stderr_contains: \"nonexistent.txt\"\n    traces:\n      - prd005-wc R6.2\n","numLines":211,"startLine":1,"totalLines":211}}}
